As I said previously, I'm gathering material for a paper on NetHack and 
its community.  One of the things I'm finding that I'd like to focus on 
is the twin questions of spoilers and save-scumming.

I know save-scumming is something of a touchy issue; what I'm 
particularly curious about is how the anti-scumming policy came about, 
and what folks' stances are on it.  I believe I've even seen some folks 
mention that they play exclusively on NAO to avoid the possible 
temptation of scumming.

As for spoilers, the situation is the reverse, from what I can tell: 
it's generally socially acceptable to ask for help or use the spoilers 
to succeed (or at least survive) in NetHack.  The situation reminds me 
greatly of something that's been discussed in the class I'm writing this 
paper for - James Paul Gee's concept of "distributed knowledge" diffused 
within a community.  NetHack seems to be almost impossible to win by 
oneself - it's a single-player game that's almost too complicated for 
any player acting entirely alone to figure out.  The key word there is 
seems;  I've noted a few people here mentioning that they avoid looking 
at spoilers and/or asking for help, because they prefer the challenge of 
attempting NetHack solo.  Is believed to be possible to ascend without 
the help of spoilers?  Are there any who claim to have done so?

What I find interesting (and refreshing) is that NetHack and its 
community seem to take the opposite stance on these two issues when 
compared to most other (non-Roguelike) games/game communities; this 
model seems somewhat more realistic (in that in real life, one is free 
to consult friends and other reference sources for help, but one cannot 
simply rewind time to reverse a mistake).  Though NetHack obviously 
isn't trying for realism in every aspect (it *is* a fantasy game, after 
all), it seems to have more internally consistent logic than most games 
I've played.

...apologies.  I'm practically writing my essay straight to the 
